diagnosis.rootCause:
The eight landmarks were built from generic props on unshaped Perlin terrain. HeightAt could only apply noise, the Wasting flattening and water carving. It had no way to level, raise, sink or step the ground under a landmark, so:
- the toll gate's gap sat on a mound;
- the Eyrie sat at ground level;
- Umbra scarp was cliff boxes on flat meadow;
- the wallow was plain grass.

There were also no builders for a gate, keep, cave, rib, nest or shrine. The data reused house-like or placeholder kinds instead:
- the Ash-Walker caves used "crypt" (a slate-roofed cottage);
- the Salt-trader's stall used striped variant 1;
- the ribs and the tear shrine used "ruin" (upright boxes and a stepped block);
- the brood was just a camp on bare ground.

Fix:
1. A small data-driven terrain-shaping system (ZoneShape, applied inside HeightAt). A flat pad is levelled to the mean natural height round its edge, plus an optional height for a raised perch or a sunk basin. A step raises the ground on one side of a line to make a real scarp. Each shape can optionally paint the ground (mud, rock, shade, unmade), and painted ground grows no grass.
2. New prop builders in the existing primitive style: gate, keep, perch, scarp, wallow, cave, shelter, brazier, brood, rib, spine, shrine, idol, with helpers Arc, Mask, Glowing and Bone. The Salt-trader's stall gets a hide-and-bone variant 3.
3. Data changes in peaks.json and ashrim.json. Every quest-relevant name, position and interaction is kept: Purple-tear shrine, the 6 Petrified ribs, Salt-trader's stall, Toll-house strongbox, camps and residents.

Two knock-on fixes:
- Walls now reach down to the lowest ground along their run, because the toll walls go from the levelled pad onto natural ground.
- Zone boundary colliders are sized to the tallest ground on each edge, because the Umbra shelf lifts the west edge about 8 m and would otherwise rise above the old 11 m boundary.

Lore labels in code comments:
- CANON: caves of the Eastern Ridge (book1 ch.20), bone masks wired with iron (book1 ch.10), Weave-Eaters made of frayed Weave threads (ch.20), the petrified ribcage as an image (ch.20).
- GAME-ONLY: the purple tear used early, all the looks, the Sandthrone banner colours, the brood.

